<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<EncodingOptions xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
  <!--
    Seeded by boot2deb's `jellyfin-v4l2request` feature. Jellyfin rewrites this file
    on every start, so these are starting values, not enforced ones: change them
    here for the next image, or on the running server for this one.

    Only the fields that differ from Jellyfin's own defaults appear. Anything
    absent keeps the default, including EncoderPreset, the CRF values, throttling,
    and AllowHevcEncoding (off — HEVC output is a per-site decision about what the
    clients can play, not a property of the hardware).
  -->

  <!--
    The absolute prefix path, not the /usr/bin/ffmpeg-rk symlink. Jellyfin takes no
    ffprobe path; it derives one by replacing the last component of this one. This
    path yields /opt/ffmpeg-rk/bin/ffprobe, which the ffmpeg-rk package ships.
  -->
  <EncoderAppPath>/opt/ffmpeg-rk/bin/ffmpeg</EncoderAppPath>

  <!--
    Decode and encode on different stacks, which the server this image builds can
    express and a stock one cannot. HardwareAccelerationType is the pairing's family
    for anything that reads it directly; the two fields after it override it.

    Decode is on the V4L2 stateless decoders, reached through the request API
    (`-hwaccel v4l2request`). Encode is the VEPU580 through MPP (`h264_rkmpp`,
    `hevc_rkmpp`). The rkmpp *decoders* find no MPP decode client on a mainline
    kernel, which is why decode must not follow HardwareAccelerationType here.
  -->
  <HardwareAccelerationType>rkmpp</HardwareAccelerationType>
  <HardwareDecodingType>v4l2request</HardwareDecodingType>
  <HardwareEncodingType>rkmpp</HardwareEncodingType>
  <EnableHardwareEncoding>true</EnableHardwareEncoding>

  <!--
    What rkvdec decodes on the RK3588, at 8 and 10 bits. FFmpeg tries the decoder
    with the widest frame-size range first, so all three land on rkvdec. A 10-bit
    frame is unpacked from NV15 on the CPU. AV1 is a separate hantro-vpu decoder with known
    deterministic gaps on some streams; add it only once those have been checked
    against the content in question.
  -->
  <HardwareDecodingCodecs>
    <string>h264</string>
    <string>hevc</string>
    <string>vp9</string>
  </HardwareDecodingCodecs>

  <!--
    ffmpeg-rk is built without OpenCL, so there is no hardware tone-map to reach
    and enabling either of these would only cost CPU on a software path.
  -->
  <EnableTonemapping>false</EnableTonemapping>
  <EnableVppTonemapping>false</EnableVppTonemapping>
</EncodingOptions>
